Here's a few personal observations about omega.  (Omega, incidentally, is
a rogue/hack/moria type game that has gone through three releases, and has
currently been abandoned by the author.  I am, on and off, working on 
redoing major sections of it to fix bugs and to increase modifiability.
Maybe in five years or so I'll be able to release it. :-)

In article <yorkw.658381456@stable.ecn.purdue.edu> yorkw@stable.ecn.purdue.edu (Willis F York) writes:
>i personally like the way Omega does the Dungons. Ya see the layout better.
>inventory seems to be a litle smoother, as does Wearing/Weilding stuff.
>tunnling is a LOT easier. then a)pply p)ick Dir.

Omega does tend to have a much more logical interface in many ways.  In fact,
omega tends to have a lot of neat ideas that never got carefuly carried
through in the code.  The author himself states that he tended to emphasize
broadening the scope of the game rather than fixing bugs, and as a result
there are many bugs that can make the game no fun to play.

>Summary. Nethack is a Better game But the Omega "interface" is better in
>my definition. seems easier to tell what ya stats are and such,
>Scrolloing Dungons Cool too.

Interesting point - the omega interface is *nasty* over anything going at
less than 9600 baud.  It does all sorts of excessive redraws, and having
to scroll the dungeon itself (for the unitiated, the omega dungeon level
is about four screens in size) is a pain.

>I don't want to start a flame war. But i se how NH could be a little better.

Any game can be better, and of all the games out there, nethack is probably
the one that is best supported.  There is a devoted development team that
is constantly fixing bugs and expanding the game.  Anyways, that's my
two cents worth!

	  - Paul

PS:  Has anybody ever looked at the scroll code?  It has if/then's to give
you different messages when you read a scroll while you're blind!  Makes
a lot of sense to me, since you can't read a scroll while you're blind!
